course: dictionary
lesson: 12556
title: Devoid of something
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Devoid means completely without something. It has none of that thing at all, not even a little. «The desert was devoid of water.» That means there was no water anywhere in the desert, not one drop.
explain: Devoid is always followed by "of". Ask yourself: is there a little of it, or none? Devoid says none. «Space is devoid of air» means there is no air in space, so you could not breathe there.
explain: Devoid is a formal word, so you will meet it in science and in careful writing. A speech «devoid of meaning» is a speech with no meaning in it. The words fill the time, but they say nothing.
fact: Devoid means completely without something.
fact: Devoid is always followed by "of": devoid of life, devoid of humour.
fact: «The room was devoid of furniture.» This means there was no furniture at all.
fact: Another way to say devoid of is "totally without" or "with none of".
fact: Devoid is a formal word, so it is most common in books, science and serious writing.
exercise: «The desert was devoid of water.» What does devoid of water mean? | Had water only at night | Had a little water | *Had no water at all | Was full of water
exercise: «The speech was devoid of meaning.» What does devoid mean here? | *Completely without | Slightly lacking | Full of | Very loud
exercise: Which word in this list means completely without? | bright | clouds | sky | *devoid
test: «The classroom was devoid of books.» What does this tell you? | *There were no books at all | There were books on every desk | There were a few books | There were lots of books
test: Which sentence means there were no trees at all? | The forest was full of trees | *The forest was devoid of trees | The forest had some trees | The forest had trees near the path
exam: «Her story was devoid of any kind of humour.» What does this mean? | It was a very funny story | It had a little humour in it | *It had no humour at all | It was a sad story about humour
